# Upstream C++ members that deliberately have no binding on the downstream surface.
#
# Read by check_api_drift.ps1, which fails on any public member of a mapped class that
# reaches no binding. Format, one per line:
#
#     <pair id>  <Type>::<Member>     # why it is not exposed
#
# The key is the PAIR plus the member, so exempting something from the C ABI says
# nothing about whether a COM server should carry it. That distinction is the reason
# there are three of these files across the three repositories rather than one shared
# list: MsgFacade answers a different question about the same class than Msgcore_c.h
# does, and a shared exemption would quietly answer both.
#
# A REASON IS NOT DECORATION. The C API is a deliberate subset, and the argument this
# repository has never written down anywhere is WHICH subset and why. Each line here is
# one sentence of that argument, and the next reader should be able to tell whether it
# still holds. "Internal" on its own is not a reason; "takes a P2PmsgHANDLE, which is
# not a type the flat surface has" is.
#
# UNTRIAGED marks a line banked by -Seed on the day the check was first run. It means
# only that nobody has looked yet. The count is printed on every run for the reason the
# register keeps relearning -- a number nobody prints is a number nobody reads -- and
# working it down is the actual product of this check.
#
# THERE ARE NONE LEFT. The seed run banked 159 and the block holding them stood at the
# top of this file for eleven sections of stack_paths.md; it is gone, and the arguments
# that replaced it are at the bottom, under "THE BACKLOG". The count is still printed at
# zero, because -Seed can put a hundred and fifty back with one switch and the run that
# does it should say so out loud.
#
# There is one thing this file cannot do: it cannot notice that a binding's BEHAVIOUR
# drifted from the C++ it wraps. msgcore_mgr_load began refusing stored images that
# 0278981's walks now judge, with no signature change and no line here. Only a test
# catches that, and none did.



# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  TRIAGED -- decided, with the reason the header asks for.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  HeapHolders is the WORKING of IsSole, not a second answer. IsSole asks "can a
#  write through this be seen anywhere but here", and answers it by comparing the
#  heap's reference count against the references this object and the sub-objects it
#  owns are holding; HeapHolders is that second number. The count on its own is only
#  meaningful next to P2PmsgHeap_RefCount, which the flat surface does not expose, and
#  it takes a P2PmsgHANDLE, which is not a type the flat surface has.
#
#  THIS REASON SAID ONE THING THAT WAS NOT TRUE, and section 34 is where that was
#  measured. It read "a caller on the flat surface wants the answer and has it --
#  IsSole is the question the C API would carry". The caller does not have it: there
#  is no msgcore_field_is_sole in Msgcore_c.h, and none of the nine
#  msgcore_field_is_* predicates answers this question. IsSole scored BOUND only
#  through check_api_drift.ps1's prefix fallback, which rooted 'is_sole' at 'is' and
#  matched msgcore_field_is_null. The argument above survives the correction whole --
#  the working of a question is not the question -- but whether the flat surface
#  should carry the question itself was left open rather than settled. Refer
#  stack_paths.md sections 29 and 34; the fallback no longer scores it, and IsSole
#  carries a line of its own at the bottom of this file.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::HeapHolders    # the working of IsSole, and takes a P2PmsgHANDLE
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::HeapHolders    # as P3PmsgField::HeapHolders, one level in
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::HeapHolders    # as P3PmsgField::HeapHolders, one level in
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgCurs::HeapHolders    # as P3PmsgField::HeapHolders; P3PmsgCurs has no flat surface at all
cxx-to-c  MsgStck::HeapHolders    # as P3PmsgField::HeapHolders, one level in
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::HeapHolders    # as P3PmsgField::HeapHolders, plus the data cursors the list caches
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::HeapHolders    # as P3PmsgField::HeapHolders, plus the element cursor the vect caches
#  P3PmsgData::HeapHolders, added by the same section, needs no line: P3PmsgData has
#  no family in api-drift.config.psd1 and is therefore never scanned. The config says
#  why in its own comment -- the class has no C projection by design.
#
#  Drop frees the item in the store; destroy releases a HANDLE to it. The flat
#  surface already spells the second, once per family -- msgcore_field_destroy,
#  msgcore_attr_destroy, msgcore_desc_destroy, msgcore_list_destroy,
#  msgcore_vect_destroy -- so an export named msgcore_vect_drop sitting beside
#  msgcore_vect_destroy would be two names that read the same and do the opposite.
#  The surface removes things the other way round: it asks the PARENT and names the
#  child (msgcore_field_delete_item, msgcore_attr_delete, msgcore_desc_delete,
#  msgcore_list_delete_at, msgcore_vect_delete), or it empties in place
#  (msgcore_*_truncate). Both spellings leave every handle the caller is holding
#  valid. Drop asked of the item ITSELF cannot: it frees the block other live
#  handles alias, and the durable re-resolution route rule 2 of Msgcore_c.h offers
#  against relocation -- hold the p2pos, come back through msgcore_mgr_p2pos2field
#  -- resolves to nothing once the item is gone. The flat ABI has no way to tell a
#  handle that it died, so it does not hand out the call that kills one.
#
#  Two notes, because "drop is internal" would not have been a reason here. First,
#  P3PmsgVect::Drop was written by d2763ce for exactly one caller -- MsgStck::Pop
#  freeing the generation it had just unlinked, "no vect had ever been a pushed
#  stack generation" (MsgVect.cpp:585) -- and that caller IS on the flat surface, as
#  msgcore_stck_pop, which reaches Drop from the inside. Second, msgcore_stck_drop
#  is not a counter-example: "Release the stacked position without restoring it"
#  (Msgcore_c.h:886) frees no item the caller holds a handle on and unlinks nothing
#  from the tree.
#
#  P3PmsgList::Drop (MsgList.h:122) is the same member under the same reason, and it
#  spent one section with no line here BECAUSE IT COULD NOT CARRY ONE: the prefix
#  fallback scored it BOUND against msgcore_list_drop_head -- the root was
#  'msgcore_list_drop' and msgcore_list_drop_head starts with it -- so a line would
#  have come back reported as redundant and the next reader told to delete it.
#  msgcore_list_drop_head and _drop_tail bind P3PmsgList::DropHead and ::DropTail,
#  which take one element off an end, and the tightened matcher now says so: a
#  function that is already another member's exact binding is not evidence for this
#  one. The line below is that reason promoted out of this comment, where section 34
#  parked it.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::Drop    # frees the item; every drop-shaped name on the flat surface releases a handle instead
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::Drop    # as P3PmsgVect::Drop; msgcore_list_drop_head and _drop_tail are DropHead's and DropTail's
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::Drop    # as P3PmsgVect::Drop; the surface deletes from the parent, by name
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::Drop    # as P3PmsgVect::Drop, of a collection; msgcore_attr_truncate empties one without freeing it
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::Drop    # as P3PmsgVect::Drop, of a collection; msgcore_desc_truncate empties one without freeing it
#
#  == and != on a field ask IDENTITY -- the same heap handle and the same block
#  address, and therefore the SAME ITEM -- and not value equality, which is r_data()
#  and r_name(). Refer stack_paths.md section 21. The flat surface already carries
#  that question, as a VALUE rather than as a predicate: msgcore_field_get_p2pos is
#  "the natural inode number (st_ino)" (Msgcore_c.h:547), and two handles denote the
#  same item exactly when their p2pos are equal and non-zero, compared with the
#  caller's own ==. It is the STRONGER of the two tests, which is why exposing the
#  operator would make the surface worse rather than wider: the operator compares a
#  block ADDRESS, and rule 2 at the top of Msgcore_c.h says any allocating call may
#  move every block, so a msgcore_field_equals would be answerable only between two
#  handles re-resolved since the last mutation, where the p2pos comparison survives
#  one. The residue is small and worth naming: p2pos returns 0 for an unaddressable
#  standalone field, so two distinct FLOATING fields compare equal on the flat
#  surface where the operator would separate them.
#
#  The name comparison this same name also carries, operator == ( LPCTNAM ), is
#  answered by msgcore_field_get_name plus the caller's own compare, and by
#  msgcore_field_exists for the lookup form. Neither half of the name is missing
#  from the surface; both are spelled as something other than an operator.
#
#  != is here and == was UNTRIAGED for no reason but timing. 4f4c334 added both
#  overloads on the same day. == was already a banked NAME, because
#  operator == ( LPCTNAM ) predates it (P2Pmsg.h:720 at 4f4c334^), and the check
#  asks about each distinct name once, so the new == collapsed into the line that
#  already existed and never resurfaced; != was a new name and surfaced at once.
#  They are triaged together because they are one decision.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::operator!=    # identity, which msgcore_field_get_p2pos already carries as a comparable value
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::operator==    # as operator!=; and the LPCTNAM overload is msgcore_field_get_name
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  The thirty-five the tightened matcher surfaced.
#
#  check_api_drift.ps1's prefix fallback used to root a C++ member at its first
#  snake-cased segment and take whatever the surface listed first, which scored
#  GetHeadPos against msgcore_list_get_count and IsSole against
#  msgcore_field_is_null. Section 34 measured that -- 43 of 116 "bound" came
#  through the fallback and 28 of those rooted at a bare accessor verb -- and
#  deliberately left it, because tightening it turns silent passes into questions
#  about the C API and that is a session of its own. This is that session. The
#  honest bound count is 81, not 116, and the thirty-five members below are the
#  difference. None of them was decided before today; every one of them was
#  reported green.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#  A LIST POSITION IS A RAW ARENA OFFSET, AND THE ABI CANNOT HAND ONE OUT.
#  GetHeadPos and GetTailPos return VBLaddr, and VBLaddr is `#define VBLaddr
#  UINT_PTR` (Msgcore.h:49) -- an offset into the heap image, pointer-wide, which
#  Msgcore.h:71 says to "key off pointer width on every toolchain". Two things
#  follow and either alone is enough. It is not a stable VALUE: rule 2 at the top
#  of Msgcore_c.h says any allocating call may move every block, and a position
#  held across one addresses whatever now occupies that offset -- the same defect
#  as a stale p2pos, without p2pos's re-resolution route through
#  msgcore_mgr_p2pos2field. And it is not a stable TYPE: an export returning
#  UINT_PTR makes the flat ABI pointer-width-dependent, which is the x86/x64 split
#  section 33 spent a section on.
#
#  So the surface walks a list the two other ways, and both are re-validated on
#  every call. By ordinal -- msgcore_list_get_int_at, _get_wstr_at, _set_int_at,
#  _delete_at, all bounded by msgcore_list_get_count -- and by cursor, the whole
#  msgcore_curs_ family, which owns the position rather than handing it to the
#  caller. GetNext, GetPrev and GetTail are the same walk from the other end: they
#  return P3PmsgData&, a reference into the live object, and P3PmsgData has no
#  family in api-drift.config.psd1 at all because it has no C projection by design.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::GetHeadPos    # returns VBLaddr, a pointer-wide arena offset rule 2 invalidates; the surface indexes by ordinal
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::GetTailPos    # as GetHeadPos, from the other end
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::GetNext    # the VBLaddr walk, and it returns P3PmsgData&, which the surface does not carry
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::GetPrev    # as GetNext, backwards
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::GetTail    # returns P3PmsgData&; the surface reads element get_count-1 by ordinal
#
#  BLOCK GEOMETRY IS THE ARENA'S BUSINESS AND MFC'S POSITION IS NOT A C TYPE.
#  GetVBLockType takes a POSITION (MsgList.h:125, MsgVect.h:101), which is MFC's
#  list cookie and exactly the case this file's own header names -- "takes a
#  P2PmsgHANDLE, which is not a type the flat surface has" -- and it answers with
#  the storage block's type code, the uVBLockDefs nibble section 36 documents for
#  the image scanner. GetVBLockListSize and GetVBLockVectSize answer with the
#  block's byte size.
#
#  The surface asks the neighbouring question and asks it about CONTENT rather
#  than storage: msgcore_list_get_type_at and msgcore_vect_get_type report an
#  element's data type, msgcore_field_get_data_type a field's, and
#  msgcore_type_name / msgcore_type_from_name give that vocabulary names. A block
#  type code has no such vocabulary on the flat side, and publishing one would put
#  the arena's header layout into the supported ABI, where every future change to
#  the format becomes a breaking change.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::GetVBLockType    # takes an MFC POSITION and answers with a block type code; the surface reports data types
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::GetVBLockType    # as P3PmsgList::GetVBLockType
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::GetVBLockListSize    # the storage block's byte size; arena layout, not message content
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::GetVBLockVectSize    # as GetVBLockListSize
#
#  THE LIBRARY'S OWN HANDLES ARE NOT THE SURFACE'S HANDLES. P2PmsgFieldHdl is
#  three bare UINT_PTRs (P2PmsgVBLock.h:276-281) and its list and vect twins are
#  the same shape; P2PmsgHANDLE is the heap handle the HeapHolders block above
#  already refuses for being no type of the surface's. MsgFieldHandle and its
#  siblings are opaque by construction: a caller obtains one from
#  msgcore_mgr_root, _p2pos2field, _field_child or _from_field, and returns it
#  through the matching _destroy. Exporting a getter for the internal struct would
#  publish a pointer-width record of heap handle and block address -- unowned,
#  undestroyable, and invalid after the next allocation -- beside the opaque handle
#  the rules are written for. There would then be two handle vocabularies in one
#  ABI and only one set of rules.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::GetP2PmsgFieldHdl    # returns three raw UINT_PTRs; the surface's handles are opaque and caller-owned
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::GetP2PmsgListHdl    # as GetP2PmsgFieldHdl
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::GetP2PmsgVectHdl    # as GetP2PmsgFieldHdl
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::GetP2PmsgHandle    # returns P2PmsgHANDLE, the heap handle, which is not a type the flat surface has
#
#  AN EXPORT THAT RETURNS BITS NOBODY CAN NAME CANNOT BE USED. GetAccess masks
#  VBLockField::uAccessAttr (P2Pmsg.cpp:4279-4283) and GetPermissions masks
#  VBLockDesc::uPermissions and VBLockAttr::uPermissions (MsgDesc.cpp:773-779).
#  The access byte at least has a vocabulary -- AttrField_HIDDEN, _NOCOPY, _SORT,
#  _TOUCH, _EXPAND (P2PmsgVBLock.h:283-289) -- but it is declared in a header
#  Msgcore_c.h does not include and a C caller does not have, so a
#  msgcore_field_get_access would answer with a UCHAR whose bits the caller cannot
#  spell. The permission byte is worse: grep the repository and nothing names a
#  single bit of it anywhere.  Exporting either means exporting the constants too,
#  and the constants are the arena's on-disk semantics, which is the commitment
#  the block-geometry reason above declines for the same reason.
#
#  SetAccess was here and its two SetPermissions counterparts were left UNTRIAGED
#  when this block was written, for a reason with a shelf life: SetAccess surfaced
#  with that batch and they did not, and moving lines nobody had asked about would
#  have moved the UNTRIAGED count out from under the sections quoting it. Those
#  sections are written and the figure is theirs now, so the two lines join the
#  argument they always belonged to.
#
#  The writing half is the worse half, and not only by symmetry. Both setters take
#  an ADD mask and a REMOVE mask and push them straight into the stored byte --
#  pDesc->uPermissions &= ~uRemove; |= uAdd (MsgDesc.cpp:763-770, MsgAttr.cpp:711-718)
#  -- so a C caller who cannot name a bit cannot name it in either argument, and the
#  failure is silent: the wrong bit does not fail the call, it changes what the
#  stored object permits. And the setter ALLOCATES. Both call Create() when the
#  collection does not exist yet, which is a heap mutation, which under rule 2 of
#  Msgcore_c.h invalidates every live handle the caller is holding -- an
#  msgcore_desc_set_permissions would be a settings call with the blast radius of a
#  declare, for a vocabulary the surface cannot publish.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::GetAccess    # masks a block-header byte whose bit names live in a header the C ABI does not publish
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::SetAccess    # as GetAccess, writing; the same unspellable bits
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::GetPermissions    # masks uPermissions, a byte no header in this repository names a single bit of
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::GetPermissions    # as P3PmsgDesc::GetPermissions
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::SetPermissions    # as GetPermissions, writing add/remove masks of unnamed bits, and it allocates on the way
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::SetPermissions    # as P3PmsgDesc::SetPermissions
#
#  THE DIRTY FLAG BELONGS TO THE C++ OBJECT, NOT TO THE ITEM. P3PmsgField::IsDirty
#  is m_bFieldDirty || P3PmsgData::IsDirty() || P3PmsgName::IsDirty()
#  (P2Pmsg.cpp:4293-4300) and the vect's adds m_bVectDirty (MsgVect.cpp:1111-1117).
#  Every one of those is an in-memory flag on the wrapper, set by writes made
#  THROUGH THAT WRAPPER, and nothing in the block records it. Two flat handles onto
#  the same item are two C++ objects with two flags, so a msgcore_field_is_dirty
#  would answer "has anything been written through this particular handle since it
#  was made" -- a question about the handle's own short life, which rule 2 already
#  tells callers to keep short.
#
#  The question that survives handles being minted and destroyed is the store's,
#  and the surface carries it: msgcore_mgr_is_dirty and msgcore_mgr_set_dirty,
#  which is what a caller deciding whether to msgcore_mgr_save actually needs.
#
#  P3PmsgList::IsDirty (MsgList.cpp:713-719) is m_bListDirty || P3PmsgField::IsDirty(),
#  the same shape one class over, and it was UNTRIAGED when this block was written
#  only because the old matcher scored it against msgcore_list_get_count through the
#  'is' root and the tightened one surfaced the field's and the vect's first. It is
#  the same decision and it joins them here. P3PmsgBSTR::IsDirty is NOT the same
#  question -- it asks P2PmsgHeap_IsDirty of the image's own heap handle
#  (P2PmsgBSTR.cpp:457-463) -- and is covered with the rest of that class below.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::IsDirty    # a flag on the C++ wrapper, not on the item; msgcore_mgr_is_dirty outlives a handle
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::IsDirty    # as P3PmsgField::IsDirty, plus m_bVectDirty
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::IsDirty    # as P3PmsgField::IsDirty, plus m_bListDirty
#
#  THE PARENT POINTER IS SOMETHING THE FLAT CALLER ALREADY HAS. GetField returns
#  P3PmsgField* -- the owning field of the collection (MsgDesc.h:211, MsgAttr.h:182).
#  On the flat surface a collection handle can only have come from a field handle,
#  through msgcore_desc_from_field or msgcore_attr_from_field, so the caller is
#  holding the answer before it asks. Handing it back would also break the one rule
#  the handle group states without exception -- "Returned handles are caller-owned
#  (msgcore_field_destroy)" (Msgcore_c.h:523-524) -- because this one is not: it
#  aliases the collection's owner, and destroying it would take down the tree the
#  collection is standing in.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::GetField    # the owning field, which a flat caller already holds: msgcore_desc_from_field is the only way in
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::GetField    # as P3PmsgDesc::GetField
#
#  SELECT BY VALUE AND SELECT BY REFERENCE ARE ONE CALL IN C. Select returns
#  P3PmsgField by VALUE and SelectItem returns P3PmsgField& (MsgDesc.h:158-165,
#  MsgAttr.h:131-138); the pair exists because C++ callers choose between a
#  detached copy and an alias into the collection. The flat surface has no such
#  choice to offer: msgcore_desc_select_item and msgcore_attr_select_item return an
#  opaque handle the caller owns and destroys, which is already the by-value form,
#  and rule 2 governs it either way. A second export would differ from the first in
#  nothing a C caller could observe.
#
#  SelectObject is refused for a different reason and a harder one: it returns
#  P3PmsgObject, and P3PmsgObject is one of the four classes api-drift.config.psd1
#  keeps out of TypeMap on purpose, because it has no C projection by design. The
#  surface answers "what kind of thing is this" the way section 20 settled it --
#  msgcore_field_is_list, _is_vect, _is_attr, _is_desc, _is_null, _is_void asked of
#  a field handle -- rather than by handing over an untyped node.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::Select    # by-value select; msgcore_desc_select_item already returns an owned handle, which is the same thing in C
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::Select    # as P3PmsgDesc::Select
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::SelectObject    # returns P3PmsgObject, a class the config keeps off the surface by design
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::SelectObject    # as P3PmsgDesc::SelectObject
#
#  IsName WAS DECLARED AND NEVER DEFINED, AND THE DECLARATION IS GONE. MsgVect.h
#  declared it beside IsData, IsField, IsList and IsVect while MsgVect.cpp defined
#  those four and not this one; the whole repository contained no P3PmsgVect::IsName
#  body, and nothing called it, which is the only reason the DLL ever linked. The
#  four siblings bind exactly, to msgcore_vect_is_data, _is_field, _is_list and
#  _is_vect. Removing a declaration that has never had a working caller cannot break
#  a consumer that compiles today -- one that wrote the call already met LNK2019 --
#  so it was deleted rather than defined, and the member is no longer scanned. This
#  block is kept as the record of why there is no line, because the next reader to
#  notice the gap in the family will otherwise ask the same question again.
#
#  A PATH IS A MANAGER'S QUESTION. GetPath returns a CString built by walking from
#  this field up to the root, and CString is an MFC type the flat surface does not
#  have. The surface asks the same question of the manager and answers in wchar_t*:
#  msgcore_mgr_p2pos2path, reached with msgcore_field_get_p2pos -- two calls that a
#  field-side export would collapse into one only by giving every field handle a
#  manager it does not carry.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::GetPath    # returns CString; msgcore_field_get_p2pos + msgcore_mgr_p2pos2path is the flat spelling
#
#  GetP2Pos HAD A LINE HERE AND NO LONGER NEEDS ONE. It read "bound as
#  msgcore_field_get_p2pos; the snake-caser spells it get_p2_pos and cannot match",
#  and it said, in as many words, that no sequence of general rules turns one into
#  the other without inventing a dictionary. That was wrong, and cheaply so. There
#  is a rule: a word boundary is a CASE change, and a digit belongs to the word it
#  sits in, so GetP2Pos is get_p2pos the same way P2Pos2Field is p2pos2field. The
#  old first rule ended a word at a digit. ConvertTo-Snake is fixed, the measurement
#  is in check_api_drift.ps1 beside it -- 44 of 288 members change spelling, three
#  gain an exact binding, none loses one -- and three lines left this file:
#  P3PmsgField::GetP2Pos from here, and P2PmsgMgr::P2Pos2Field and ::P2Pos2Path out
#  of the UNTRIAGED backlog, where they had been banked as work outstanding since
#  the day the check was seeded. They were bound the whole time.
#
#  That is the shape worth keeping: a matcher's false NEGATIVE reads exactly like an
#  honest gap, and it inflates the backlog rather than the bound count, which is the
#  flattering direction and therefore the one nobody audits.
#
#  PageRegistration IS BOUND UNDER A DIFFERENT NAME, TWICE. The two overloads
#  (P2PmsgMgr.h:211-215) install the page-in/page-out pair and the populate
#  callback, and the surface spells them msgcore_mgr_set_paging_sinks and
#  msgcore_mgr_set_populate_sink (Msgcore_c.h:1027-1032), with msgcore_pagein_fn,
#  msgcore_pageout_fn and msgcore_populate_fn as the C signatures of the three
#  P2Page*CBFnc types. The rename is deliberate and documented -- the C header
#  spends fifteen lines on how a sink must behave and how it differs from the
#  trigger sink -- so the missing binding is the matcher's, not the surface's.
#  PageRegistrationPush and PageRegistrationPop bind exactly and are a different
#  pair of calls.
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::PageRegistration    # bound as msgcore_mgr_set_paging_sinks and msgcore_mgr_set_populate_sink; a rename the matcher cannot follow
#
#  THE PATH GRAMMAR HAS NO FLAT SPELLING, AND THAT IS THIS INVESTIGATION'S OWN
#  SUBJECT. RootPath2Object takes an absolute object path and resolves it, which
#  means it implements the grammar sections 2 to 5 and 17 to 19 are about: the
#  leading '.' root marker, '.' descent, '@' into attributes, and '^' into the
#  pushed generation. The flat surface carries no path parser at all. It descends
#  one named step at a time -- msgcore_mgr_root, then msgcore_field_child or
#  msgcore_field_select_item -- and it has no spelling whatever for '@' or '^',
#  which are the two components that make the grammar more than string
#  concatenation.
#
#  Exporting a path resolver would therefore not be adding a convenience; it would
#  commit the supported ABI to a grammar whose corner cases this document needed
#  thirty-odd sections to pin down, and every one of those cases would become an
#  ABI promise. msgcore_mgr_p2pos2path goes the other way -- position to text --
#  and commits to nothing, which is why it is there and this is not.
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::RootPath2Object    # resolves the '.'/'@'/'^' path grammar; the surface descends a step at a time and spells neither '@' nor '^'
#
#  A MANAGER-SIDE PREDICATE KEYED ON A POSITION IS TWO CALLS ON THE FLAT SIDE.
#  IsField ( P2Pos ) and IsAttributed ( UCHAR, P2Pos ) ask about the object at a
#  position rather than about the manager, so the flat caller resolves first --
#  msgcore_mgr_p2pos2field -- and then asks the field: msgcore_field_is_list and
#  msgcore_field_is_vect settle IsField between them, and
#  msgcore_field_is_attributed answers the unmasked half of IsAttributed. The
#  masked half is the access byte again, and the reason it is not exported is the
#  one two blocks up: the bits have no names on this side of the boundary.
#
#  Two calls instead of one is the shape the whole handle group is written in --
#  obtain, use, discard -- and a manager-side shortcut would have to hold a
#  resolved field across the caller's next mutation, which rule 2 forbids.
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::IsField    # p2pos2field then msgcore_field_is_list / _is_vect; the surface asks the item, not the manager
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::IsAttributed    # as IsField; msgcore_field_is_attributed answers the unmasked half and the mask is the unnamed access byte
#
#  IsSole AND IsInline: NOT CARRIED, AND THE DECISION IS THE OWNER'S TO REVISIT.
#  These are the two members section 34 named. IsSole asks whether a write through
#  this field can be seen anywhere but here and its TRUE is a guarantee (section 26,
#  narrowed by sections 29 and 31); IsInline asks whether the block lives inside the
#  field object, where its TRUE is a guarantee and its FALSE is not (section 24).
#  Both scored BOUND against msgcore_field_is_null through the old 'is' root, which
#  is how section 29's HeapHolders reason came to say a flat caller "wants the
#  answer and has it". It does not have it. There is no msgcore_field_is_sole and no
#  msgcore_field_is_inline, and none of the nine msgcore_field_is_* predicates
#  answers either question.
#
#  The case FOR carrying IsSole is real and should be written down rather than
#  argued away. msgcore_field_is_sole ( MsgFieldHandle ) would be
#  signature-identical to the nine predicates already there, it needs no type the
#  surface lacks, and its TRUE is the one fact a flat caller cannot reconstruct from
#  anything else on the surface: p2pos answers identity, not exclusivity, and there
#  is no flat route to P2PmsgHeap_RefCount. A TreeFS host deciding whether it may
#  write in place has exactly this question.
#
#  The case AGAINST is that it is an ADDITION TO THE SUPPORTED ABI. Msgcore_c.h is
#  the 282-function surface the README calls supported; a new export bumps
#  Msgcore_version.h in the same commit, and that is a versioning decision belonging
#  to whoever owns the release, not to a check that reads names. There is a second
#  half: IsSole's FALSE still promises nothing (section 26's asymmetry, unmoved by
#  sections 29 and 31, and the per-block reference count is still a different
#  library), so the export would be a predicate that is load-bearing in one
#  direction and advisory in the other, and the header would have to say so in as
#  many words. IsInline is the same shape and weaker: its FALSE is explicitly not a
#  guarantee (section 24), and it describes where storage sits rather than what the
#  caller may do, which is the block-geometry line this file has already drawn twice.
#
#  So: not carried, today, by this file. The lines below record that, not a refusal
#  in principle. Adding msgcore_field_is_sole remains a live option and the argument
#  for it is above, in full, on purpose.
#
#  AND IT WAS TAKEN. IsSole no longer has a line here, because msgcore_field_is_sole
#  is on the supported surface: Msgcore_c.h:296-345, with fifty lines of contract
#  saying what the case AGAINST demanded it say -- TRUE is a guarantee, FALSE is
#  "unknown" and not evidence of a second holder -- and a version bump to 3.1.0
#  beside it. The check reported the line redundant on the run after it landed and
#  the line is gone. Writing the case FOR down in full is what made that a decision
#  somebody could take rather than a thing a matcher had silently scored green since
#  the day it was seeded, which is the whole argument of this file in one member.
#
#  IsInline keeps its line and keeps it for the reason the paragraph above gives:
#  there is no msgcore_field_is_inline, its FALSE is explicitly not a guarantee
#  (section 24), and it describes where storage sits rather than what a caller may
#  do. The two were never the same strength and only one of them was worth exporting.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::IsInline    # no msgcore_field_is_inline; its FALSE guarantees nothing and it describes storage placement, not permission
#
# ===================================================================================
#  THE BACKLOG -- the 159 lines -Seed banked on day one, argued.
#
#  Everything above this point was written about a member somebody had already
#  looked at. The block that used to stand at the TOP of this file was the opposite:
#  159 lines saying only that nobody had, carried through eleven sections of
#  stack_paths.md as the largest single thing outstanding. It is gone from the file
#  because it is decided below, and two of its lines left for a better reason than a
#  decision -- the matcher learned to read them and they had been bound all along.
#
#  The arguments are the product, not the lines. Fifteen of them cover the rest,
#  which is the same point section 34 made with four Drops and section 37 made with
#  four groups over thirty-five members: a backlog needing one argument per line is
#  a backlog nobody has understood yet. Where a member IS carried under a name the
#  matcher cannot follow, the line says so and cites the wrapper source, because the
#  one thing worse than an unexplained gap is a claimed gap that is not there.
# ===================================================================================
#
#  1. A CONSTRUCTOR IS A NAME C CANNOT SPELL, AND A DESTRUCTOR IS ONE CALL PER
#  FAMILY. C has neither overloading nor destructors, so the surface spells
#  construction as a named factory per shape and destruction as exactly one function
#  per family: msgcore_mgr_create / _create_nn / _open_file, msgcore_field_create /
#  _clone / _child, and a _from_field for attr, desc, list, vect, stck and recurs
#  (curs takes three: _from_field, _from_attr, _from_desc). Those ARE the
#  constructors. msgcore_field_destroy is literally the destructor -- it deletes the
#  wrapper (Msgcore_c.cpp:369-376) -- and the only reason the check does not report
#  it is that the C++ name snake-cases to ~p3pmsg_field.
#
#  What the surface declines is not construction but most of the argument lists. The
#  check asks about each distinct NAME once, so P3PmsgField's seven constructors
#  arrive as one line and that line has to answer for the hardest of them: two are
#  carried (the empty one as msgcore_field_create, name+data as a declare), the copy
#  is carried as msgcore_field_clone -- "heap-allocated copies the caller must
#  destroy", Msgcore_c.h:180-186 -- and the remaining four take P2PmsgFieldHdl,
#  P2PmsgHANDLE + VBLaddr + VBLsize, P3PmsgData& or P3PmsgObject&: every one a type
#  this file has already refused, in the GetP2PmsgFieldHdl block and the GetHeadPos
#  block above. The collections repeat the pattern exactly, and P3PmsgBSTR's nine
#  are a case of their own, argued at the end.
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::P2PmsgMgr    # four ctors; msgcore_mgr_create / _create_nn / _open_file are three of them, the copy takes a P2PmsgMgr&
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::~P2PmsgMgr    # run by msgcore_mgr_destroy, which deletes the wrapper
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::P3PmsgField    # seven ctors; _create / _clone / a declare carry three, the rest take P2PmsgHANDLE, VBLaddr or an off-surface class
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::~P3PmsgField    # run by msgcore_field_destroy (Msgcore_c.cpp:369-376)
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::P3PmsgList    # seven ctors; msgcore_list_from_field is the one that needs no refused type
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::~P3PmsgList    # run by msgcore_list_destroy
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::P3PmsgVect    # as P3PmsgList::P3PmsgList; msgcore_vect_from_field, and the sized forms take a P3PmsgData&
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::~P3PmsgVect    # run by msgcore_vect_destroy
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::P3PmsgAttr    # four ctors; msgcore_attr_from_field is the one, the others take P3PmsgField* or P3PmsgObject&
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::~P3PmsgAttr    # run by msgcore_attr_destroy
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::P3PmsgDesc    # as P3PmsgAttr::P3PmsgAttr; msgcore_desc_from_field
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::~P3PmsgDesc    # run by msgcore_desc_destroy
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgCurs::P3PmsgCurs    # five ctors, which the surface spells as three: msgcore_curs_from_field / _from_attr / _from_desc
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgCurs::~P3PmsgCurs    # run by msgcore_curs_destroy
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgRecurs::P2PmsgRecurs    # three ctors; msgcore_recurs_from_field and _from_attr are two, the third is the copy
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgRecurs::~P2PmsgRecurs    # run by msgcore_recurs_destroy
cxx-to-c  MsgStck::MsgStck    # four ctors; msgcore_stck_create and _from_field are two, the others take P3PmsgField* or P3PmsgObject&
cxx-to-c  MsgStck::~MsgStck    # run by msgcore_stck_destroy
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::P3PmsgBSTR    # nine ctors, every one taking VBListIOmage, P2PmsgHANDLE or P3PmsgData&; see the P3PmsgBSTR block below
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::~P3PmsgBSTR    # as P3PmsgBSTR::P3PmsgBSTR; the surface spells no msgcore_bstrio_ function at all
#
#  2. ASSERTVALID THROWS, AND NOTHING THROWN CROSSES THIS BOUNDARY.
#  P3PmsgField::AssertValid (P2Pmsg.cpp:4119-4133) runs VerifyContainment and, when
#  it fails, EVERR -> Message("Failed Containment") -> Throw(). That is the one
#  behaviour a flat entry point may not have: Msgcore_c.cpp catches 111 times, and
#  every wrapper that can reach C++ converts a throw into a return code or a NULL
#  handle. An msgcore_field_assert_valid would either swallow the diagnosis, leaving
#  a bool that says less than the assertion did, or let an exception cross an extern
#  "C" frame.
#
#  The surface already asks the question the safe way. msgcore_mgr_is_valid binds
#  P2PmsgMgr::IsValid exactly -- same question, int instead of a throw -- and
#  section 35 is the section about what happens when an assertion fires inside a
#  gate that was supposed to be watching for it.
#
#  VerifyContainment is the predicate underneath AssertValid and it takes a raw
#  blob pointer and a VBLsize, asking whether a span lies inside this object's
#  VBLock. Both halves are refused elsewhere in this file: a caller-side pointer
#  into the arena, and the arena's own size type. Print is the odd one, because its
#  signature (FILE*, int, int) is C-compatible and its argument has to be about
#  something else: it dumps the block layout section 36 and section 40 read with
#  their own tooling, in a format nothing versions, and the flat ABI has no
#  diagnostic-output group for it to belong to. Adding one would make a debug
#  format a supported one.
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::AssertValid    # throws on failure; msgcore_mgr_is_valid is the same question with a return value
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::AssertValid    # throws on failure (P2Pmsg.cpp:4119-4125); an exception cannot cross extern "C"
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::AssertValid    # as P3PmsgField::AssertValid
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::AssertValid    # as P3PmsgField::AssertValid
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::AssertValid    # as P3PmsgField::AssertValid
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::AssertValid    # as P3PmsgField::AssertValid
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgCurs::AssertValid    # as P3PmsgField::AssertValid
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgRecurs::AssertValid    # as P3PmsgField::AssertValid
cxx-to-c  MsgStck::AssertValid    # as P3PmsgField::AssertValid
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::AssertValid    # as P3PmsgField::AssertValid
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::VerifyContainment    # takes a raw blob pointer and a VBLsize; it is AssertValid's predicate, and both types are refused above
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::VerifyContainment    # as P3PmsgField::VerifyContainment
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::VerifyContainment    # as P3PmsgField::VerifyContainment
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::VerifyContainment    # as P3PmsgField::VerifyContainment
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::VerifyContainment    # as P3PmsgField::VerifyContainment
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::Print    # dumps block layout to a FILE*; a debug format, and exporting it would make it a supported one
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::Print    # as P2PmsgMgr::Print
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::Print    # as P2PmsgMgr::Print
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::Print    # as P2PmsgMgr::Print
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::Print    # as P2PmsgMgr::Print
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::Print    # as P2PmsgMgr::Print
cxx-to-c  MsgStck::Print    # as P2PmsgMgr::Print
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::Print    # as P2PmsgMgr::Print
#
#  3. ASSIGNMENT IS FIVE DIFFERENT CALLS WEARING ONE SPELLING. operator= collapses
#  to one NAME upstream and the check asks about it once, but P3PmsgField declares
#  five of them (P2Pmsg.h:748-756) and each is a different question on the flat
#  side. From a P3PmsgField it is the deep copy, which IS carried, as
#  msgcore_field_clone. From a P3PmsgData it is a data write -- msgcore_field_set_int,
#  _set_wstr, _set_double, _set_bool, _set_int64, one per type because C has no
#  P3PmsgData. From a P3PmsgName it is a rename, msgcore_field_rename_child. From a
#  P2PmsgFieldHdl it adopts three raw UINT_PTRs, refused above. From a P3PmsgObject
#  it takes a class the config keeps off the surface by design.
#
#  So the capability is on the surface four times over and the OPERATOR is not,
#  which is the outcome the identity-operator block above reached by a different
#  route: the surface spells questions as verbs. The collections repeat the shape
#  with their own container types; P2PmsgMgr's assigns a whole store, which on the
#  flat side is msgcore_mgr_save followed by msgcore_mgr_open_file.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::operator=    # five overloads; the copy is msgcore_field_clone, the data write is the _set_* family, the rename is _rename_child
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::operator=    # as P3PmsgField::operator=; the data arm is msgcore_list_set_*_at
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::operator=    # as P3PmsgField::operator=; the data arm is msgcore_vect_set_*
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::operator=    # as P3PmsgField::operator=; the other arm takes P3PmsgObject, which has no C projection
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::operator=    # as P3PmsgAttr::operator=
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgCurs::operator=    # re-seats a cursor on another item; the flat cursor is re-seated by destroying it and calling msgcore_curs_from_field again
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgRecurs::operator=    # as P3PmsgCurs::operator=, for the recursive walker
cxx-to-c  MsgStck::operator=    # copies a stack wrapper; the flat one is minted by msgcore_stck_from_field and released by _destroy
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::operator=    # assigns a whole store; the flat spelling is msgcore_mgr_save then msgcore_mgr_open_file
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::operator=    # see the P3PmsgBSTR block below; the class has no family on the surface
#
#  4. `if ( oField )` IS msgcore_field_is_void, NEGATED. P3PmsgField::operator bool
#  is exactly !IsVoid() (P2Pmsg.cpp:3928-3931), and the comment above it says the
#  spelling was deliberately narrowed to that one question so that `if ( oField )`
#  means the same thing on the field, the list and the vect. Section 20 is the
#  section about the four answers this used to have. msgcore_field_is_void carries
#  it, and a C caller has the second half free: the handle is NULL or it is not.
#
#  The list's and the vect's are the same !IsVoid() one class over (MsgList.cpp:383),
#  and a flat caller reaches it on the field it must already be holding -- a list
#  handle can only have come from msgcore_list_from_field. Two calls, which is the
#  shape the whole handle group is written in.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::operatorbool    # !IsVoid(); msgcore_field_is_void carries it, plus the handle's own NULL
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::operatorbool    # as P3PmsgField::operatorbool, asked of the field the list handle came from
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::operatorbool    # as P3PmsgList::operatorbool
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::operatorbool    # as P3PmsgList::operatorbool; msgcore_attr_is_empty is the neighbouring question the surface does carry
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::operatorbool    # as P3PmsgAttr::operatorbool; msgcore_desc_is_empty
#
#  5. operator[] THROWS WHEN THE NAME IS NOT THERE, AND THE SURFACE RETURNS NULL.
#  P3PmsgAttr::operator[] goes to the collection's cached cursor and, if Goto fails,
#  EVERR -> Message("Item [%s] does not exist") -> Throw() (MsgAttr.cpp:214-224);
#  P3PmsgField::operator[] is r_Desc()[lpszName] (P2Pmsg.cpp:3910-3913) and inherits
#  the same behaviour one level down. The flat surface answers the identical
#  question in two calls that cannot throw: msgcore_attr_exists / msgcore_desc_exists
#  for the test, msgcore_attr_select_item / msgcore_desc_select_item / msgcore_field_child
#  for the fetch, each returning NULL rather than raising. That is not a narrower
#  capability; it is the same one with the error path an ABI can carry.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::operator[]    # r_Desc()[name], which throws on a miss; msgcore_field_child and _desc_select_item return NULL instead
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::operator[]    # throws on a miss (MsgAttr.cpp:219); msgcore_attr_exists + _select_item is the flat spelling
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::operator[]    # as P3PmsgAttr::operator[]
#
#  6. THE SURFACE BUILDS IN PLACE AND NEVER ADOPTS A CALLER-BUILT OBJECT.
#  operator+= and PushBack are one member with two spellings -- P3PmsgAttr's three
#  += overloads are each a one-line call to PushBack (MsgAttr.cpp:196-212) -- and
#  what they do is graft an existing C++ object into a collection. There is no flat
#  counterpart because every flat construction call is BY NAME AND VALUE:
#  msgcore_attr_declare_int, _declare_wstr, _declare_list, _declare_vect and their
#  desc twins make the child inside the collection and hand back a handle to it.
#
#  The one thing the surface does graft is a node that is already in the tree, by
#  name, between two live handles: msgcore_field_move_child(hSrc, hDst, lpszName),
#  "rename(2) across directories" (Msgcore_c.h:593-596). The distinction is exactly
#  the one rule 2's exemption note draws (Msgcore_c.h:112-116): a handle a C caller
#  is holding may be a DETACHED deep copy whose block is not in the heap at all, so
#  an msgcore_attr_push_back would have to copy that block into the arena -- which
#  is the value copy sections 23 and 27 are about, and it would have to decide
#  there what those sections show the C++ side decides badly.
#
#  P3PmsgList::operator+= is the one arm with a flat name: += a P3PmsgData is
#  msgcore_list_add_tail_int / _double / _wstr / _bool / _int64, spelled per type
#  because C has no P3PmsgData. Its other arm concatenates a whole list and has
#  none, for the reason above.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::PushBack    # grafts a caller-built object; the surface declares by name and value (msgcore_attr_declare_*)
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::PushBack    # as P3PmsgAttr::PushBack
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::operator+=    # three overloads, each a call to PushBack (MsgAttr.cpp:196-212)
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::operator+=    # as P3PmsgAttr::operator+=
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::operator+=    # appends a list, vect or field object; the surface spells it msgcore_field_declare_list / _declare_vect
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::operator+=    # the data arm IS carried, as msgcore_list_add_tail_*; the list-concatenation arm adopts an object and is not
#
#  7. A CONVERSION OPERATOR RETURNS A CLASS, AND THE FLAT SIDE RETURNS A HANDLE.
#  P3PmsgCurs has five (MsgCurs.h:121-129) and they are how C++ reads the element
#  under a cursor. The flat cursor does it with one function and a type test:
#  msgcore_curs_is_item / _is_list / _is_vect, then msgcore_curs_get_field.
#
#  And one of these five IS the binding, from the inside. msgcore_curs_get_field is
#  implemented as *pResult = (P3PmsgField&)(*toCurs(hCurs)) (Msgcore_c.cpp:1141-1145)
#  -- that cast IS operator P3PmsgField&, and the assignment beside it is the
#  operator= two blocks up. The flat surface reaches both on every call; what it
#  does not do is hand out the reference, because the three remaining conversions
#  yield P3PmsgName&, P3PmsgData& and P3PmsgList& / P3PmsgVect&, and the first two
#  are classes api-drift.config.psd1 keeps out of TypeMap on purpose. The same
#  precedent as msgcore_stck_pop reaching Drop from the inside, in section 34.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgCurs::operatorP3PmsgField&    # reached from the inside by msgcore_curs_get_field (Msgcore_c.cpp:1143); the surface returns a handle, not a reference
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgCurs::operatorP3PmsgList&    # as operatorP3PmsgField&; msgcore_curs_is_list then _get_field then msgcore_list_from_field
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgCurs::operatorP3PmsgVect&    # as operatorP3PmsgList&, for a vect
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgCurs::operatorP3PmsgName&    # returns P3PmsgName&, a class with no family in the config by design
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgCurs::operatorP3PmsgData&    # returns P3PmsgData&, a class with no family in the config by design
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::operatorP3PmsgData&    # as P3PmsgCurs::operatorP3PmsgData&; the values are read by msgcore_field_get_int / _get_wstr / ...
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::operatorP3PmsgData&    # as P3PmsgField::operatorP3PmsgData&; msgcore_list_get_*_at reads the element
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::operatorP3PmsgData&    # as P3PmsgField::operatorP3PmsgData&; msgcore_vect_get_* reads the element
#
#  8. THE STEP OPERATORS ARE CARRIED AS VERBS, AND ONE OF THEM IS NOT CARRIED AT
#  ALL. Msgcore_c.h documents msgcore_recurs_next as "Advance (operator++)" in as
#  many words (Msgcore_c.h:942-944), and msgcore_curs_next sits under the heading
#  "Advance / rewind" beside msgcore_curs_seek, which binds P3PmsgCurs::Seek
#  exactly. So the forward step is on the surface twice; only the spelling is
#  different, and it has to be, because ++ is not a name in C.
#
#  operator-- has no counterpart: there is no msgcore_curs_prev. A flat caller steps
#  back with msgcore_curs_goto_index(msgcore_curs_item_index(c) - 1), which is what
#  Seek() does one step further -- Seek is Goto(0) (MsgCurs.cpp:324-328) -- and the
#  absolute index is the position the flat cursor is willing to talk about, for the
#  reason the GetHeadPos block gives about VBLaddr. It is O(n) per step where the
#  C++ operator is O(1), and Msgcore_c.h:716-720 says that plainly about the list's
#  indexed access. That cost is the reason to reconsider it, and it is recorded
#  here rather than argued away.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgCurs::operator++    # carried as msgcore_curs_next; ++ is not a name C can export
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgRecurs::operator++    # carried as msgcore_recurs_next, which Msgcore_c.h:942 names "(operator++)"
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgCurs::operator--    # no msgcore_curs_prev; the flat step back is goto_index(item_index-1), which is O(n)
#
#  9. r_<thing> IS CARRIED AS get_<thing>, AND THE RENAME IS NOT A RULE.
#  The library's convention is that r_x() exposes a reference to x. The flat
#  surface's convention is get_x(), returning a handle the caller owns. For fourteen
#  members those are the same function, and the wrapper source says so line by line:
#
#      MsgStck::r_item   -> msgcore_stck_get_item      (Msgcore_c.cpp:2142)
#      MsgStck::r_list   -> msgcore_stck_get_list      (Msgcore_c.cpp:2150)
#      MsgStck::r_vect   -> msgcore_stck_get_vect      (Msgcore_c.cpp:2158)
#      MsgStck::r_name   -> msgcore_stck_get_name      (Msgcore_c.cpp:2120)
#      P2PmsgRecurs::r_item / r_list / r_vect / c_wstr -> msgcore_recurs_get_item /
#                                    _get_list / _get_vect / _get_name  (2263-2283, 2252)
#      P3PmsgCurs::c_wstr -> msgcore_curs_get_name     (Msgcore_c.cpp:1152)
#      P3PmsgVect::r_item / r_list / r_vect / r_name / r_data -> msgcore_vect_get_item /
#                                    _get_list / _get_vect / _get_name / _get_int
#                                    and the rest of the typed family  (806, 1986, 1996, 1932, 783)
#
#  A RULE MAPPING r_ TO get_ WAS TRIED AND REJECTED ON MEASUREMENT, which is worth
#  recording because the same session fixed ConvertTo-Snake by finding a rule. This
#  one binds nine members correctly and three WRONGLY: msgcore_curs_get_name is
#  P3PmsgCurs::c_wstr's (1152) and msgcore_recurs_get_name is P2PmsgRecurs::c_wstr's
#  (2252), so a rule handing them to r_name -- which returns P3PmsgName& and is not
#  carried -- would manufacture exactly the kind of false positive section 37
#  removed twenty-eight of; msgcore_field_get_name is c_name's (Msgcore_c.cpp:510)
#  and would be taken from it the same way. Three false bindings to save nine lines
#  is the wrong trade, and the nine lines can say which function they mean.
cxx-to-c  MsgStck::r_item    # bound as msgcore_stck_get_item (Msgcore_c.cpp:2142); r_ is the surface's get_
cxx-to-c  MsgStck::r_list    # bound as msgcore_stck_get_list (Msgcore_c.cpp:2150)
cxx-to-c  MsgStck::r_vect    # bound as msgcore_stck_get_vect (Msgcore_c.cpp:2158)
cxx-to-c  MsgStck::r_name    # bound as msgcore_stck_get_name, which returns r_name().c_name() (Msgcore_c.cpp:2120)
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgRecurs::r_item    # bound as msgcore_recurs_get_item (Msgcore_c.cpp:2263)
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgRecurs::r_list    # bound as msgcore_recurs_get_list (Msgcore_c.cpp:2273)
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgRecurs::r_vect    # bound as msgcore_recurs_get_vect (Msgcore_c.cpp:2283)
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgRecurs::c_wstr    # bound as msgcore_recurs_get_name (Msgcore_c.cpp:2252)
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgCurs::c_wstr    # bound as msgcore_curs_get_name (Msgcore_c.cpp:1152)
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::r_item    # bound as msgcore_vect_get_item (Msgcore_c.cpp:806)
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::r_list    # bound as msgcore_vect_get_list (Msgcore_c.cpp:1986)
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::r_vect    # bound as msgcore_vect_get_vect (Msgcore_c.cpp:1996)
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::r_name    # bound as msgcore_vect_get_name (Msgcore_c.cpp:1932)
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::r_data    # bound as the typed element getters: msgcore_vect_get_int is r_data(nElem).c_int() (Msgcore_c.cpp:783)
#
#  10. THE OTHER r_ MEMBERS HAND OUT A C++ SUB-OBJECT, WHICH IS THE ONE THING A
#  HANDLE CANNOT BE. Three distinct cases, none of them "internal".
#
#  First, the inheritance seam. P3PmsgField::r_data is `return (P3PmsgData&)*this`
#  (P2Pmsg.cpp:3936-3940): the field IS its data and its name by derivation, and
#  r_data / r_name expose the base sub-objects. The flat surface reads through the
#  field handle itself, with no intermediate object -- msgcore_field_get_int is
#  c_int() (Msgcore_c.cpp:538) and msgcore_field_get_name is c_name() (:510) -- and
#  P3PmsgData, P3PmsgName, P3PmsgItem and P3PmsgObject are the four classes
#  api-drift.config.psd1 keeps out of TypeMap on purpose.
#
#  Second, the collection's OWN cursor. r_Curs returns the P3PmsgCurs a collection
#  caches and reuses (m_pCurs, allocated on first use at MsgAttr.cpp:216-217), which
#  is the object section 31 is about. The flat surface never lends it: every
#  msgcore_curs_from_attr / _from_desc / _from_field mints a fresh cursor the caller
#  owns and destroys, precisely so that one caller's walk cannot move another's
#  position. Handing out the cached one would make two flat callers share a cursor
#  through handles neither of them can see the other holding.
#
#  Third, the two-call shape. P3PmsgCurs::r_attr is m_pP3PmsgFoN->r_Attr()
#  (MsgCurs.cpp:615-621) -- the attributes of the element under the cursor -- and
#  the flat route is msgcore_curs_get_field then msgcore_attr_from_field. Same for
#  r_desc, r_list, r_vect and r_item, and for P3PmsgField::r_Stck, which is
#  msgcore_stck_from_field. That is not a workaround; it is the same "obtain, use,
#  discard" the handle group states as its rule.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::r_data    # returns (P3PmsgData&)*this (P2Pmsg.cpp:3939); the surface reads through the handle, msgcore_field_get_int is c_int()
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::r_name    # the P3PmsgName base sub-object; msgcore_field_get_name is c_name() (Msgcore_c.cpp:510)
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::r_Object    # returns P3PmsgObject&, a class the config keeps off the surface by design
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::r_Object    # as P3PmsgField::r_Object
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::r_Object    # as P3PmsgField::r_Object
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgCurs::r_Object    # as P3PmsgField::r_Object
cxx-to-c  MsgStck::r_data    # returns P3PmsgData&; the flat read is msgcore_stck_get_item then a typed field getter
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgRecurs::r_data    # as MsgStck::r_data, through msgcore_recurs_get_item
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgRecurs::r_name    # returns P3PmsgName&; the text is msgcore_recurs_get_name, which is c_wstr's binding
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgCurs::r_data    # as MsgStck::r_data, through msgcore_curs_get_field
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgCurs::r_name    # as P2PmsgRecurs::r_name; msgcore_curs_get_name is c_wstr's
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgCurs::r_item    # returns P3PmsgItem&; msgcore_curs_get_field hands back an owned handle instead
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgCurs::r_list    # msgcore_curs_is_list + _get_field + msgcore_list_from_field
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgCurs::r_vect    # as P3PmsgCurs::r_list, for a vect
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgCurs::r_attr    # the element's attributes (MsgCurs.cpp:621); flat, that is _get_field then msgcore_attr_from_field
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgCurs::r_desc    # as P3PmsgCurs::r_attr, with msgcore_desc_from_field
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgRecurs::r_attr    # as P3PmsgCurs::r_attr; it delegates to the walker's own cursor (MsgCurs.cpp:864-868)
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::r_Attr    # carried as msgcore_attr_from_field(hField, bCreate); AttrCMD_Create IS that flag
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::r_Desc    # carried as msgcore_desc_from_field; the wrapper calls r_Desc(AttrCMD_Create) to serve it (Msgcore_c.cpp:1576)
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::r_Stck    # carried as msgcore_stck_from_field, which mints an owned stack rather than lending the field's cached one
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::r_Curs    # lends the collection's cached cursor (MsgAttr.cpp:216); msgcore_curs_from_attr mints a caller-owned one instead
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::r_Curs    # as P3PmsgAttr::r_Curs, with msgcore_curs_from_desc
#
#  11. A FLAT HANDLE HAS NO DISCONNECTED STATE TO MOVE TO -- EXCEPT ON THE ONE
#  FAMILY WHERE IT DOES, WHICH IS HOW THIS ARGUMENT CHECKS ITSELF. Connect and
#  Nullify are the C++ wrapper's two-state life: Nullify drops the parent pointer
#  and deletes the cached cursor (MsgAttr.cpp:109-116), Connect is Nullify followed
#  by re-pointing (:117-121). On the flat side a collection handle comes into
#  existence connected -- msgcore_attr_from_field is the only way to get one, and
#  Msgcore_c.h:378 describes it in exactly these words, "Connect to the attribute
#  collection of hField (Create=true allocates if absent)" -- and leaves existence
#  through _destroy. There is no third state for a connect call to reach.
#
#  MsgStck is the exception and it proves the rule rather than breaking it:
#  msgcore_stck_create makes an UNCONNECTED stack, so the family needs both
#  msgcore_stck_connect and msgcore_stck_nullify, and MsgStck::Connect and ::Nullify
#  bind exactly and appear nowhere in this file. P2PmsgMgr::Nullify is the same and
#  binds msgcore_mgr_nullify.
#
#  P3PmsgField, P3PmsgList and P3PmsgVect refuse for a second reason on top of the
#  first: their Connect takes (P2PmsgHANDLE, VBLaddr, VBLsize), which is the heap
#  handle and the raw arena offset this file has already refused twice.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::Connect    # takes P2PmsgHANDLE + VBLaddr + VBLsize; and a flat handle is minted connected by msgcore_field_child / _p2pos2field
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgList::Connect    # as P3PmsgField::Connect; msgcore_list_from_field mints a connected handle
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgVect::Connect    # as P3PmsgField::Connect; msgcore_vect_from_field
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::Connect    # msgcore_attr_from_field IS the connect (Msgcore_c.h:378); there is no unconnected flat attr handle to re-point
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::Connect    # as P3PmsgAttr::Connect, with msgcore_desc_from_field
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::Nullify    # the wrapper's disconnected state, which a flat handle never has; _destroy is the only exit
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::Nullify    # as P3PmsgField::Nullify; compare msgcore_stck_nullify, which exists because msgcore_stck_create makes one unconnected
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::Nullify    # as P3PmsgAttr::Nullify
#
#  12. Create IS AN ARGUMENT, NOT A FUNCTION. P3PmsgAttr::Create allocates the
#  attribute VBLock in the owning field when there is not one yet (MsgAttr.cpp:236-248).
#  The flat surface carries it as the second parameter of the call that would have
#  needed it: msgcore_attr_from_field(hField, bCreate) calls pAttr->Create() when
#  bCreate is set (Msgcore_c.cpp:825-830), and msgcore_desc_from_field does the same
#  at :930-935. Exporting a separate msgcore_attr_create would give a caller a way to
#  allocate -- a rule 2 mutation, invalidating its own live handles -- as a call that
#  reads like a constructor and is not one.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgAttr::Create    # carried as the bCreate argument of msgcore_attr_from_field (Msgcore_c.cpp:830)
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgDesc::Create    # carried as the bCreate argument of msgcore_desc_from_field (Msgcore_c.cpp:935)
#
#  13. THE MANAGER'S ADDRESSING CALLS, AFTER TWO OF THEM TURNED OUT TO BE BOUND.
#  P2Pos2Field and P2Pos2Path were in the backlog until the snake-caser was fixed;
#  they are msgcore_mgr_p2pos2field and msgcore_mgr_p2pos2path and they always were.
#  The three that remain are the three whose RETURN TYPE the surface does not have.
#  P2Pos2Attr returns a P3PmsgAttr, which flat is two calls -- p2pos2field, then
#  msgcore_attr_from_field -- the shape the IsField block above already argued.
#  P2Pos2Object and Path2Object return P3PmsgObject, one of the four classes the
#  config keeps off TypeMap by design, and Path2Object additionally resolves the
#  path grammar, which is the RootPath2Object argument two blocks up: the flat
#  surface descends a named step at a time and spells neither '@' nor '^'.
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::P2Pos2Attr    # returns P3PmsgAttr; flat that is msgcore_mgr_p2pos2field then msgcore_attr_from_field
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::P2Pos2Object    # returns P3PmsgObject, a class the config keeps off the surface by design
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::Path2Object    # as RootPath2Object: it resolves the path grammar, and returns P3PmsgObject as well
#
#  14. THE TRIGGER FAMILY WAS DELIBERATELY RE-KEYED FOR THE FLAT SURFACE, AND THE C
#  HEADER SAYS SO. Msgcore_c.h:660-663 opens the group with "The legacy trigger
#  facility posts a Windows message to an HWND when an armed node changes. These
#  wrappers add the windowless path a FUSE/daemon host needs": one function-pointer
#  sink per manager, nodes armed by P2Pos. So msgcore_mgr_create_trigger and
#  _drop_triggers take (mask, p2pos) where the C++ takes (mask, HWND, p2pos), and
#  they bind CreateTrigger and DropTriggers exactly.
#
#  The four here are what is left over from that re-keying. SelectTrigger asks which
#  mask is registered for a node AND A WINDOW; with no HWND in the flat vocabulary
#  there is nothing for the second argument to be. TriggerINSERT, TriggerUPDATE and
#  TriggerDELETE fire a specific type for an object the caller is holding, and the
#  surface spells all three as ONE function with the type as a mask --
#  msgcore_mgr_fire_trigger(hMgr, MSGCORE_TRIGGER_INSERT, p2pos) -- which exists
#  precisely because the C++ form needs a P3PmsgItem in hand that a FUSE writer does
#  not have (Msgcore_c.h:684-694). The capability is carried three times over; the
#  three names are not, and cannot be without putting the C++ overload set back.
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::SelectTrigger    # keyed on an HWND, which the flat trigger group deliberately dropped (Msgcore_c.h:660-663)
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::TriggerINSERT    # carried as msgcore_mgr_fire_trigger with MSGCORE_TRIGGER_INSERT; the flat form is keyed on a P2Pos, not a P3PmsgItem
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::TriggerUPDATE    # as TriggerINSERT, with MSGCORE_TRIGGER_UPDATE
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::TriggerDELETE    # as TriggerINSERT, with MSGCORE_TRIGGER_DELETE
#
#  15. THE MANAGER'S THREE CONSTRUCTION HELPERS, ONE OF WHICH DOES NOTHING AT ALL.
#  Attacheap allocates the root item's chunk straight from the P2PmsgHeap before the
#  heap is attached to the manager -- its own comment says exactly that
#  (P2PmsgMgr.cpp:123-134) -- and its only callers anywhere are P2PmsgMgr's four
#  constructors (P2PmsgMgr.cpp:39, 48, 57, 67). So the flat surface reaches it on
#  every msgcore_mgr_create, exactly as section 34 found msgcore_stck_pop reaching
#  Drop from the inside, and it takes a P3PmsgData& besides.
#
#  Factory is the static twin of the filename constructor and returns an owning
#  P2PmsgMgr*. msgcore_mgr_open_file IS that call (new P2PmsgMgr(lpszFilename),
#  Msgcore_c.cpp:250-255); a second export would be the same capability under a
#  second name, and the flat surface has one way to make each thing on purpose.
#
#  SharedMode WAS the interesting one: it used to compare its argument to
#  m_dwSharedMode and return the argument, assigning nothing (P2PmsgMgr.cpp:519-526).
#  That no-op was recorded as a defect (What is left, §44) and is now fixed --
#  it assigns m_dwSharedMode and returns TRUE. It stays off the flat surface
#  regardless: no C caller has a use for it, since the active Load/Save paths
#  do not consult m_dwSharedMode either (see the note at their CreateFile calls).
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::Attacheap    # the ctor's heap-attach step, reached from the inside by msgcore_mgr_create; takes a P3PmsgData&
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::Factory    # msgcore_mgr_open_file is this call (Msgcore_c.cpp:253); a static returning an owning raw pointer adds nothing
cxx-to-c  P2PmsgMgr::SharedMode    # a real setter now (What is left, §44); no C caller has a use for m_dwSharedMode today
#
#  16. RewindCurs DROPS THE SUB-OBJECTS A FIELD CACHES, AND A FLAT CALLER HAS NONE
#  OF THEM. Its body deletes m_pP3PmsgAttr, m_pP3PmsgDesc and m_pMsgStck
#  (P2Pmsg.cpp:3723-3734) -- the three companions a P3PmsgField builds on demand and
#  keeps, which is section 31's subject. On the flat surface those are never the
#  field's: msgcore_attr_from_field, msgcore_desc_from_field and
#  msgcore_stck_from_field each hand the caller its own handle, and the caller drops
#  it with the matching _destroy when it is done. The flat equivalent of RewindCurs
#  is already written into every caller's control flow by rule 2.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgField::RewindCurs    # deletes the field's cached attr/desc/stck companions (P2Pmsg.cpp:3723); flat callers own those handles themselves
#
#  17. P3PmsgBSTR IS A FAMILY THE SURFACE NEVER SPELLED. api-drift.config.psd1 maps
#  the class to msgcore_bstrio_, and Msgcore_c.h contains not one function with that
#  prefix -- which is why none of these nineteen is even a REFUSAL in the check's
#  tally: there is nothing under the root for the fallback to throw out.
#
#  The class is the exchange-format wrapper. A P3PmsgBSTR holds a VBListIOmage,
#  typedef'd P2Piomage (P2PmsgBSTR.h:58-69), which is a #pragma pack(push) struct
#  with a sync word carrying total size, addressing bits and a layout generation in
#  named bit ranges -- the wire and on-disk form of a P2Pmsg, and the thing sections
#  32 and 36 read with a scanner. Every constructor takes VBListIOmage, P2PmsgHANDLE
#  or P3PmsgData&; PrepareP2Piomage / P2Piomage / P2PiomageSize / ReleaseP2Piomage
#  hand out and reclaim that struct; VBLockBSTR_vp hands out a void* into it. To
#  export any of it is to publish the packed layout as a supported ABI, which is the
#  commitment the block-geometry block above declines for the arena's blocks, and
#  this is the same arena's transport form.
#
#  What the flat surface carries instead is the FILE path: msgcore_mgr_save,
#  msgcore_mgr_load and msgcore_mgr_open_file move a whole store to and from a named
#  file, with the format's version discipline kept on this side of the boundary.
#  THE GAP THAT LEAVES IS REAL AND IS NAMED HERE RATHER THAN GLOSSED: there is no
#  way for a C caller to serialise a store to a BUFFER it owns -- no
#  msgcore_mgr_save_to_buffer, no msgcore_mgr_load_from_buffer -- so a host that
#  wants to put a message on a socket must go through a temporary file. A pair of
#  buffer calls taking (void*, size_t) would need no P2Piomage in its signature and
#  would publish no layout. Like msgcore_field_is_sole above, that is an ADDITION to
#  the supported surface and a version bump, and belongs to whoever owns the
#  release; it is written down here so the next reader does not have to rediscover
#  it. Not carried today.
#
#  Three of these were declared and never defined -- IsFragmented, SetDefaultSizeof
#  and VBLockBSTR_vp had no body in any .cpp in the repository, P3PmsgVect::IsName's
#  case from section 37, three more times (What is left, §44). IsFragmented is now
#  deleted outright, on IsName's own precedent: there is no P2PmsgHeap primitive
#  behind it, so defining one would have been inventing a policy rather than wiring
#  an existing one. SetDefaultSizeof and VBLockBSTR_vp are now defined -- each a
#  one-line delegation, like Sizeof and IsDirty beside them -- and stay off this
#  surface for the ordinary reasons below rather than for failing to link.
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::Init    # takes P2PmsgHANDLE or P3PmsgData&; the surface spells no msgcore_bstrio_ function of any kind
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::InitData    # as Init, with a P3PmsgData&
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::InitItem    # as Init, with a VBLockBSTR type code and a P3PmsgData&
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::PrepareP2Piomage    # hands out the packed VBListIOmage; exporting it publishes the wire layout as ABI
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::P2Piomage    # as PrepareP2Piomage; the flat serialisation is file-based (msgcore_mgr_save / _load)
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::P2PiomageSize    # as P2Piomage; the size of a struct the ABI does not publish
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::ReleaseP2Piomage    # as P2Piomage, releasing
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::Exists    # keyed on a VBLockBSTR_* section code (P2PmsgBSTR.h:36-44), a vocabulary the C header does not publish
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::r_item    # keyed on the same section code, and returns P3PmsgItem&
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::r_datn    # SelectItem(TBSTR_Msg) on the image (P2PmsgBSTR.cpp:430-433); same vocabulary
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::r_name    # returns P3PmsgName&, a class with no family in the config by design
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::r_data    # returns P3PmsgData&, likewise
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::GetP2Pmsgnn    # the image's addressing-width code; the store's counterpart is msgcore_mgr_create_nn's uAddrNN, and there is no flat image
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::SetDefaultP2Pmsgnn    # a static default for the above; process-wide state the flat ABI has no call shape for
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::GetDefaultP2Pmsgnn    # as SetDefaultP2Pmsgnn, reading
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::IsDirty    # asks P2PmsgHeap_IsDirty of the image's heap handle (P2PmsgBSTR.cpp:457-463); msgcore_mgr_is_dirty is the store's version
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::SetDefaultSizeof    # returns BSTR_INITIAL_SiZE (P2PmsgBSTR.cpp), process-wide state the flat ABI has no call shape for, as SetDefaultP2Pmsgnn above
cxx-to-c  P3PmsgBSTR::VBLockBSTR_vp    # returns a void* into the packed image; exporting it publishes the wire layout as ABI, as PrepareP2Piomage above
